// SOUNDNESS QUEUE — tier 2, observed `admits`.
// FOUND 2026-09-12b by the dlog rule tools/dlog/rpit_bound_sites.dl, which
// enumerated every context holding a `LogosType::Kind::ImplTrait` value: 14
// sites in 10 contexts, of which 9 reach no bound checker. A per-site read says
// EIGHT of those nine are legitimately blind (interning, type_str, move
// marking, the mint in resolve_type, the two return-position sites that infer
// the hidden type and are covered downstream by lower_fn). The ninth is this
// one, and grep could not have separated it from the eight: they all spell
// `ImplTrait` identically.
//
// `lower_let` (sema_stmt.cpp, `ann_is_impl`) treats an `impl Trait` LET
// ANNOTATION as "any concrete type the rhs happens to have" and skips
// `expect_type` WHOLESALE. Not the kind, not the signature, not the trait:
// nothing about the annotation is checked against the initialiser. Both halves
// below compile clean and write an object file.
//
// ⚠ AND THE CONSTRUCT ITSELF IS AN UNCANONISED DIVERGENCE, WHICH IS THE OWNER'S
// AND NOT THIS ROW'S. `impl Trait` in BINDING position is not stable Rust at
// all (`impl_trait_in_bindings`), so "rustc refuses this program" is true for a
// reason that is not this defect. Neither registry carries it: no row in
// docs/DIVERGENCES.md, and docs/spec/ has clauses for the PARAMETER position
// (`type.impl-trait.param-position-forbidden`) and the RETURN position
// (`item.fn.impl-trait-return-infer`) and NONE for the binding position — while
// tests/logos/pass/impl_trait_let_ann is a green pass fixture asserting it
// works. Reported, not edited. This row is about the bound being unchecked
// INSIDE whatever the owner decides the construct means.
//
// The one-token legal contrast for the trait half is `impl Speak for A` in
// place of `impl Speak for B`; for the kind half it is `FnMut` in place of
// `Fn`, which is pinned at the RETURN spelling as
// tests/logos/pass/bc_rpitbound_kind_mut_admit.
package let_impl_trait_annotation_unchecked;
trait Speak { fn speak(&self) -> i64; }
struct A { v: i64 }
struct B { v: i64 }
impl Speak for B {
    fn speak(&self) -> i64 { return self.v; }
}
fn main() -> i32 {
    let s: impl Speak = A { v: 1i64 };
    let mut x: i64 = 0i64;
    let f: impl Fn() -> i64 = move || -> i64 { x += 1i64; return x; };
    return f() as i32;
}
